+| Symptom | Root cause | Action |
+|---|---|---|
+| "Internet connection is not working in app" | **Our bug.** `ApiConfig.BaseUrl` was the `const` `http://10.0.2.2:8080/` — the emulator's alias for the host. That address does not exist on a physical phone, and the comment claiming `adb reverse` fixed it was wrong: `adb reverse` maps the *phone's* `localhost`, not `10.0.2.2`. Every request from a phone was guaranteed to fail | Base URL now resolves at runtime from `DeviceInfo.DeviceType` (`10.0.2.2` emulator / `localhost` phone). Gate rule L fails the build if a plain-HTTP host the app uses is not permitted cleartext, or if INTERNET/ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE or the security config is missing |
+| App closes immediately after install (force close) | **Deployment, not code.** Debug builds use Fast Deployment: assemblies are kept out of the `.apk` and pushed over adb by `-t:Install`. A manually installed Debug `.apk` therefore starts with no assemblies and dies (dotnet/android#12011) | `-p:KaamLinkStandaloneApk=true` produces a self-contained Debug APK; the guide documents `adb uninstall` + `adb install` and both alternatives (`-t:Install`, `-t:Run`) |
+| "App not installed" / Play Protect calls it harmful | Debug APK signed with the local debug key and sideloaded, or a partial APK left by the failed build | Documented: uninstall first, install the `-Signed.apk` built from a *successful* build, Play Protect "Install anyway" |
+| `XAGR7028` / `XAGR7019` on `obj\…\lp\<n>\jl\res\…` | Build infrastructure: the extracted resource tree for library projects was half-written (interrupted build) or locked (Visual Studio design-time build, MSBuild, antivirus) | Close VS, end `MSBuild.exe`/`dotnet.exe`, delete `obj` and `bin`, add a Defender exclusion; documented in the guide |
+
+Hardening that came out of it, all independent of the diagnoses above:
+
+* **Theme can no longer abort startup.** `App.CreateWindow` wraps theme application; `ThemeService`
+  wraps the palette swap and the dispatched shadow refresh. A shadow refresh failing on the UI
+  thread would otherwise have been an unhandled exception — a real crash path I introduced.
+* **A crash is now diagnosable**: `AppDomain.UnhandledException` and
+  `TaskScheduler.UnobservedTaskException` log `[KaamLink] UNHANDLED EXCEPTION: …`, next to the
+  existing transport log, so `adb logcat | grep KaamLink` shows the stack.
+
+Still **BLOCKED**: nothing here has been compiled or run (no SDK/JDK/device in this environment).
+The connectivity fix is validated only by the source-level facts above and by gate rule L.
+
+### 9.16 "Not compatible" on a phone — ABI, not Android version
+
+The phone refused the app as incompatible while asking for support up to Android 17. The app has
+**no maximum Android version**: `minSdkVersion` 21 / `targetSdk` 36, and Android installs and runs
+apps built for older target SDKs on newer releases, so Android 17 was never the blocker.
+
+The real cause is the **CPU architecture inside the `.apk`**. A Visual Studio deploy builds only
+for the device selected in the deploy dropdown; deploying to an x86_64 emulator leaves an
+x86_64-only `.apk`, and an ARM phone rejects it (`INSTALL_FAILED_NO_MATCHING_ABIS`, shown to the
+user as "app not compatible").
+
+Fixed in `KaamLink.csproj`: `-p:KaamLinkStandaloneApk=true` now also sets
+`RuntimeIdentifiers=android-arm;android-arm64;android-x86;android-x64`, `AndroidPackageFormats=apk`
+(an `.aab` cannot be installed on a phone) and `AndroidCreatePackagePerAbi=false`, on top of the
+existing `EmbedAssembliesIntoApk`. One universal, self-contained `.apk` — no emulator ABIs, no
+missing assemblies.
+
+Notes that shaped the change:
+
+* `AndroidSupportedAbis` is dead since .NET 6; RIDs are the supported mechanism (dotnet/android
+  build-properties). The gate fails if it reappears.
+* On .NET 11 (CoreCLR) the workload ships no `android-arm` / `android-x86` packs
+  (dotnet/android#12172); the csproj comment says to drop those two RIDs at that point. They are
+  kept now because V1 targets affordable older handsets.
+* The 16 KB page-size guidance is a RELRO hardening nuance on this SDK (`Microsoft.Android.Sdk
+  36.1.69`, dotnet/android#13051), not an install gate: the reporter's app ran on a 16 KB device.
+  Not treated as the cause.
+
+Gate rule M now fails the build if the sideload switch loses embedded assemblies, loses the
+universal ABI list, or can emit an `.aab`; the self-test injects a single-ABI APK and detects it.
+
+### 9.17 Login-first boot, branded sign-in, and post-auth role choice
+
+The app now starts on the professional login surface rather than asking for a role before
+sign-in. A successful OTP with no role context continues to the existing Worker / Employer
+chooser; if a switch-to-role flow supplied a role before OTP, its original destination is
+preserved. Returning sessions are restored from the login entry and go to that chooser, and
+expired sessions/logout return to sign-in. The existing OTP API and role/onboarding decisions
+remain authoritative.
+
+* `Features/Auth/LoginPage.xaml` now matches the approved sign-in concept: standalone linked-K
+  brand asset, welcome hierarchy, separately bundled worker/employer SVG, Google option, +971
+  phone field, SMS OTP action, accessible error state, and consent note. The new strings have
+  EN/AR parity (325 / 325 / 325).
+* `Resources/AppIcon/appiconfg.svg` and `Resources/Splash/splash.svg` use the matching linked-K
+  mark; `Resources/Images/kaamlink-mark.svg` and `login-hero.svg` stay as separate image assets,
+  not inline image data in code.
+* **Google/Gmail authentication is not active yet.** The current API only supports
+  `POST /auth/otp/request` and `POST /auth/otp/verify`; it has no Google token-verification or
+  OAuth endpoint, and no Google OAuth client configuration exists in this repository. The
+  Google button is therefore visibly disabled with an explanation rather than pretending to
+  authenticate. Making that button live requires a real Google client configuration and a
+  server-side token-verification/exchange flow; phone OTP remains functional.
+* Verification this pass: phase03 105/0, phase04 105/0, phase05 88/0, bindings 15 pages / 319
+  types / 357 compiled bindings, UI redesign 437/0, compile surface 330/0, theme contrast 55/0,
+  strings 325/325/325, self-test 35/35 injected defects detected. The XAML/SVG/resx documents
+  parsed and `git diff --check` passed.
+* **Build/device verification BLOCKED:** this environment has no .NET SDK, JDK, emulator or
+  Android device. Static checks are not a successful build or a visual/runtime test.
